432	  color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 0.875rem; cursor: pointer;
433	  transition: background 0.15s, color 0.15s;
434	}
435	.dropdown-menu button:hover {
436	  background: var(--surface-hover); color: var(--text);
437	}
438	@keyframes dropdownIn {
439	  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-4px); }
440	  to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
441	}
442	```
443	
444	### Entrance Animations (Mandatory for All Files)
445	Every file MUST have subtle entrance animations. Static-feeling pages score low on interactivity. Use CSS-only `@keyframes` on page load:
446	```css
447	@keyframes fadeInUp {
448	  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px); }
449	  to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
450	}
451	.card, .step, .quote-card, section > * {
452	  animation: fadeInUp 0.5s ease both;
453	}
454	/* Stagger children */
455	.card:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: 0s; }
456	.card:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 0.08s; }
457	.card:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: 0.16s; }
458	.card:nth-child(4) { animation-delay: 0.24s; }
459	```
460	Also add hover states on ALL cards/items — even "static" content like cheatsheets and quote cards:
461	```css
462	.card:hover, .command-group:hover, blockquote:hover {
463	  background: var(--surface-hover);
464	  box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--border), 0 8px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);
465	}
466	```
467	
468	### Theme-Adaptive Content (Carousels, Posters)
469	When cards use decorative gradients (e.g., Instagram-style pastel slides), the gradients MUST adapt to the theme. Don't use fixed pastel colors that look identical in dark/light mode:
470	```css
471	/* BAD — same gradient in both themes */
472	.card { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ff9a9e, #fecfef); }
473	
474	/* GOOD — theme-adaptive gradients */
475	.theme-dark .card-1 { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a1a2e, #16213e); }
476	.theme-light .card-1 { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ff9a9e, #fecfef); }
477	```
478	
479	### Slide Deck Light Mode
480	Slide decks are designed dark-first, but light mode must NOT feel like an afterthought. For presentations in light mode:
481	- Use a subtle warm gray background (`#f5f5f0`) not pure white
482	- Add a faint top-down gradient for depth
483	- Ensure headings use dark text with sufficient weight
484	- Grid/glow backgrounds should switch to subtle dot patterns or soft gradients in light mode
485	
486	### Chart Accessibility (Mandatory)
487	All CSS-only charts (bars, radar, donut) and Chart.js charts MUST expose data to screen readers:
488	```html
489	<div role="img" aria-label="Bar chart showing React at 85%, Vue at 72%, Angular at 58%">
490	  <!-- visual chart here -->
491	  <div class="sr-only">React: 85%. Vue: 72%. Angular: 58%.</div>
492	</div>
493	```
494	Add `.sr-only { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0,0,0,0); }` to every file.
495	
496	### Visual Restraint Anti-Patterns (NEVER DO)
497	- ❌ **Floating gradient orbs** — decorative blurred circles behind content look amateurish
498	- ❌ **Rainbow/gradient borders** — colored top-borders or left-borders on cards scream "template"
499	- ❌ **Gradient text** on headings — use solid colors. Gradient text is a 2020 trend that aged poorly
500	- ❌ **Scale transforms on hover** — `scale(1.02)` on cards feels janky, not premium
501	- ❌ **Glow effects** — `box-shadow: 0 0 20px rgba(blue)` never looks good
502	- ❌ **Decorative animations** — spinning rings, floating particles, pulsing dots are noise
503	- ❌ **Color-coded borders** — left/top colored borders on cards feel like Bootstrap components
504	- ❌ **Stat numbers with gradient text** — use a solid accent color or var(--text) instead
505	
506	### Accessibility (Mandatory)
507	
508	Every visualization MUST meet these baseline accessibility requirements:
509	
510	**Minimum Accessibility Checklist:**
511	- [ ] Skip-to-content link present
512	- [ ] `role="region"` on all major sections with `aria-label`
513	- [ ] `role="group"` on comparison sections, architecture layers, slide groups
514	- [ ] `role="list"` / `role="listitem"` on timeline sections and items
515	- [ ] `aria-label` on all icon-only buttons
516	- [ ] `aria-describedby` on chart sections linking to data descriptions
517	- [ ] `:focus-visible` with `border-radius: 4px` on all interactive elements
518	- [ ] `aria-live="polite"` on slide counters / dynamic content
519	
520	- **Skip navigation:** Add `<a href="#main-content" class="skip-link">Skip to content</a>` at the top of `<body>`. Style: visually hidden, visible on focus.
521	- **Landmark roles:** Use `<main>`, `<nav>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<section>` with `aria-label` where there are multiple of the same landmark.
522	- **Interactive elements:** All buttons, links, and controls must have `aria-label` if their text content is not descriptive (e.g., icon-only buttons).
523	- **Focus indicators:** Add visible `:focus-visible` styles on all interactive elements: `outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px;`
524	- **Color-only indicators:** Status dots, colored badges, etc. MUST have a text alternative. E.g., a green status dot should also show "Healthy" text or `aria-label="Status: Healthy"`.
525	- **Charts and diagrams (MANDATORY):** 
526	  - Wrap chart canvas in container with `role="img" aria-label="Description of what the chart shows"`
527	  - **ALL charts MUST have hover tooltips enabled** — never disable Chart.js tooltips
528	  - Include data table alternative or visually-hidden summary for screen readers
529	  - Use high contrast colors with sufficient color difference between data series
530	- **Screen reader descriptions:** Add `aria-description` or visually-hidden text describing key takeaways for complex visualizations.
531	- **Slide decks:** Use `aria-live="polite"` on the slide counter, and `aria-label` on navigation buttons.
532	
533	### Icons (Inline SVG Only)
534	
535	Use inline SVG for all icons. **Never use emoji as icons** — they look unprofessional and render inconsistently.
536	
537	Use simple Lucide-style paths: 24x24 viewBox, stroke-based, `stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"`.
538	
539	Common icons to use:
540	- Moon/Sun for theme toggle
541	- Download arrow for PNG export  
542	- Printer for print
543	- Arrow left/right for navigation
544	- Check/X for pros/cons
545	- Globe, Smartphone, Monitor for device types
546	
547	### Animations (CSS-First with Modern Features)
548	
549	**CSS animations are the primary system.** They're reliable, performant, and never break from JS scoping issues.
550	
551	See [references/animations.md](references/animations.md) for complete patterns.
552	
553	**Modern CSS Animation Features (Progressive Enhancement):**
554	
555	**Scroll-driven animations** (Chrome 115+) — Replace JS scroll listeners:
556	```css
557	.scroll-reveal {
558	  animation: fadeInUp 1ms linear;
559	  animation-timeline: view(); /* Animates based on viewport visibility */
560	}
561	.progress-bar {
562	  animation: grow 1ms linear;  
563	  animation-timeline: scroll(root inline); /* Animates based on page scroll */
564	}
565	```
566	
567	**Three animation techniques (all baked into the skeleton):**
568	
569	1. **Page-load entrance:** Add class `animate` (+ `delay-1` through `delay-6` for stagger)
570	   ```html
571	   <h1 class="animate">Title</h1>
572	   <div class="card animate delay-1">Card 1</div>
573	   <div class="card animate delay-2">Card 2</div>
574	   ```
575	
576	2. **Scroll reveal:** Add `data-reveal` attribute. JS adds `.reveal` class (opacity:0), then `.visible` on scroll.
577	   ```html
578	   <section data-reveal>This fades in when scrolled into view</section>
579	   ```
580	
581	3. **Number counters:** Add `data-count` attribute. JS animates from 0 to target.
582	   ```html
583	   <span data-count="77" data-suffix="%">77%</span>
584	   ```
585	
586	**Hover effects** are pure CSS, baked into `.card` (translateY + scale on :hover).
587	
588	**Rules:**
589	- Content is ALWAYS visible in CSS — JS animations are progressive enhancement
590	- Use `@keyframes` for page-load animations, CSS `transition` for hover/state changes
591	- `data-reveal` elements show their final content if JS fails (no blank sections)
592	- `prefers-reduced-motion` disables all animations automatically
593	- `data-reveal` elements MUST have `opacity: 1` as their default CSS state. JS adds `.reveal` class (which sets `opacity: 0`), then `.visible` restores it. If JS fails, content stays visible.
594	- On page load, trigger ALL reveal elements visible after a short delay (500ms) so full-page screenshots and PNG exports capture all content:
595	  ```javascript
596	  setTimeout(function() { document.querySelectorAll('.reveal').forEach(function(el) { el.classList.add('visible'); }); }, 500);
597	  ```
